:"25.Nxd6","cls":"brilliant","color":"w","headline":"25.Nxd6!! A real sacrifice: White gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the knight on d6 where it can be taken.","Black can take, but after 25…Rxd6 26.Rxd6 exd6 27.Qxf6 the engine rates it +0.15.","Takes the pawn on d6.","Moving away uncovers the rook on c1, which now hits the rook on c6.","25.Nxd6 uncovers an attack on the rook on c6 and trades knight for pawn.","Trades knight for pawn."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"25.Nxd6!! offers the knight on d6. Taking it gives White the material back and more."},{"title":"What White gives up","text":"25.Nxd6 puts the knight on d6 where it can be taken by the rook on c6 and the e7 pawn. That's a knight on offer."},{"title":"If Black takes the knight","text":"25…Rxd6 is met by 26.Rxd6, taking the rook on d6. Then 27.Qxf6 takes the knight on f6, and 28.Rc7 threatens mate. Material is level and White holds the balance (+0.23). This is Black's best try."}]},{"ply":64,"name":"32…Ke8","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"32…Ke8 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 48% to 24%.","points":["White answers 33.Qxd6, which takes the pawn on d6.","After Qxd6, White wins 1 point of material within 5 moves.","Better was 32…Ke7 (+0.20), which protects the d6 pawn, which was hanging."],"better":"32…Ke7 protects the d6 pawn. After 33.Qh5 Re1+ 34.Rxe1 Qxe1+ 35.Kg2 Qe4+, the rooks are traded (+0.20)."},{"ply":73,"name":"37.Qb6","cls":"miss","color":"w","headline":"37.Qb6 lets the win slip: 37.Qb8+ kept White winning. White's winning chances go from 77% to 64%.","points":["Black answers 37…a5: the pawn on a5 can be taken by the queen on b6.","Better was 37.Qb8+ (+3.27), which protects the b3 pawn, which was hanging."],"better":"37.Qb8+ protects the b3 pawn and gives check. After 37…Ke7 38.Qb7+ Kf8 39.Kg2 a5 40.b4 Qxa4 41.Qb8+, White is a pawn down (+3.27)."}],"opening":{"name":"Zukertort Opening","eco":"A05","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 11…a6: 22 moves in all, 11 by White and 11 by Black.","Theory runs out after 11…a6: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}